WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
In 1945, Alexander Fleming, the scientist who discovered

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.280
penicillin, gave a warning in his Nobel Prize

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:07.760
lecture.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.440
If people took too little penicillin, the bacteria

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.760
that survived could become resistant.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.000
And resistance did spread.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.440
Within a few years, resistant staph bacteria were

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.160
common in some hospitals.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.960
So here's the real question.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.560
Did penicillin make the bacteria change, or were

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.880
some of them already different before the drug

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.600
arrived?

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.440
To answer it, we need three words, at

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:34.920
three different levels.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.440
A gene is a stretch of DNA that

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.080
codes for a product, like a protein.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.040
An allele is one version of that gene.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.440
Different alleles of a gene have slightly different

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:46.880
base sequences.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.360
And a gene pool is every allele of

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.920
every gene in a whole population.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.480
So a gene pool belongs to a population,

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.000
never to one organism.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.360
A mutation is a change in the base

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.840
sequence of DNA.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.680
If the change lands inside a gene, it

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.440
makes a version of that gene with a

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.480
different sequence, and that's a new allele.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.160
You've met one already.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.680
In one haemoglobin gene, one base changed, and

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.800
that made the sickle cell allele.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.560
When a change like that first appears, the

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.800
population holds a version of the gene it

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.080
didn't have before.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.620
But a new allele starts very small.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.740
A mutation happens in one cell, in one

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.780
individual, usually from a copying mistake that isn't

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.140
repaired, or from damage by a mutagen, something

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.900
that harms DNA, which is the next video.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.420
So at first, there's just one copy in

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.780
the whole population.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.980
And many mutations change nothing you can see,

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.780
because they fall outside genes, or don't change

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.660
the protein.

00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:51.706
How does that one copy spread?

00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:54.946
It depends on how the organism reproduces.

00:01:55.227 --> 00:01:58.186
A bacterium copies its DNA and splits in

00:01:58.347 --> 00:02:00.666
two, so both daughter cells get the new

00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:03.386
allele, and so does every cell descended from

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:04.186
them.

00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:07.066
In animals, a mutation is passed on only

00:02:07.147 --> 00:02:08.746
if it's in a cell that makes eggs

00:02:08.787 --> 00:02:10.026
or sperm.

00:02:10.067 --> 00:02:12.106
A mutation in a skin cell can affect

00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:15.226
that animal, but its offspring don't inherit it.

00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:18.313
Now back to Fleming's question.

00:02:18.594 --> 00:02:21.473
Mutations happen by chance, whether or not the

00:02:21.554 --> 00:02:22.433
change would help.

00:02:22.914 --> 00:02:25.553
A bacterium can't sense penicillin and make the

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:27.393
exact mutation it needs.

00:02:27.874 --> 00:02:31.313
But in a huge population, rare mutations keep

00:02:31.394 --> 00:02:34.353
turning up, so a few cells may already

00:02:34.514 --> 00:02:37.473
carry a resistance allele before any penicillin is

00:02:37.554 --> 00:02:37.953
used.

00:02:38.594 --> 00:02:41.793
Biologists say mutation is random with respect to

00:02:41.874 --> 00:02:42.353
need.

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:44.366
How do we know?

00:02:44.847 --> 00:02:49.086
In 1952, Joshua and Esther Lederberg grew bacterial

00:02:49.167 --> 00:02:52.046
colonies on a dish with no antibiotic.

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.686
They pressed a velvet pad onto it, then

00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:58.286
onto a dish containing the antibiotic streptomycin, printing

00:02:58.407 --> 00:03:00.126
the same pattern of colonies.

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:02.766
Only a few colonies grew on that second

00:03:02.847 --> 00:03:03.246
dish.

00:03:03.967 --> 00:03:05.966
Then they went back to the matching spots

00:03:06.007 --> 00:03:08.046
on the first dish, which had never touched

00:03:08.127 --> 00:03:11.246
the antibiotic, and bacteria from those spots could

00:03:11.327 --> 00:03:12.766
grow on streptomycin too.

00:03:13.327 --> 00:03:15.486
The resistance was there first.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.260
So what does penicillin actually do?

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.940
It kills the bacteria that don't carry a

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.220
resistance allele.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.820
The few that do carry one survive and

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:28.780
keep dividing, and each daughter cell inherits the

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:29.540
allele.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.340
Within days, most of the population can be

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.300
resistant.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.220
And too little penicillin also spares cells that

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.900
are only slightly resistant.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.620
The antibiotic didn't create the allele.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.580
It changed how common it is, and that's

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:44.940
natural selection.

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:49.126
Mutation isn't the only way a population's alleles

00:03:49.207 --> 00:03:51.846
change, so let's sort the processes.

00:03:52.567 --> 00:03:56.726
Meiosis and fertilisation make new combinations, but every

00:03:56.807 --> 00:03:59.126
allele in them already existed.

00:03:59.527 --> 00:04:02.326
Gene flow, when organisms or their pollen move

00:04:02.527 --> 00:04:05.286
between populations, can bring in an allele this

00:04:05.367 --> 00:04:06.886
population didn't have.

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:10.246
But somewhere, at some time, that allele was

00:04:10.327 --> 00:04:11.606
made by a mutation.

00:04:12.087 --> 00:04:15.126
So mutation is the original source of every

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:15.766
allele.

00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:19.726
So what happens to a new allele next?

00:04:20.007 --> 00:04:22.566
Many are lost by chance, harmful ones are

00:04:22.807 --> 00:04:25.526
removed by selection, and a few helpful ones

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:26.286
spread.

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:29.206
Later in this module, you'll see how selection,

00:04:29.287 --> 00:04:31.846
chance and gene flow decide which.

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:34.506
Here's a way to sort it all.

00:04:34.987 --> 00:04:37.306
Mutation makes new alleles.

00:04:37.547 --> 00:04:39.946
Meiosis and fertilisation mix them.

00:04:40.507 --> 00:04:43.146
Gene flow moves them between populations.

00:04:43.547 --> 00:04:46.266
And selection and chance change how common they

00:04:46.387 --> 00:04:46.666
are.

00:04:47.867 --> 00:04:49.306
Two exam questions now.

00:04:49.867 --> 00:04:50.346
First:

00:04:50.907 --> 00:04:54.746
A patient's infection contains about ten billion Staphylococcus

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:55.946
aureus bacteria.

00:04:56.507 --> 00:04:59.706
Before treatment, about one in every hundred million

00:04:59.787 --> 00:05:02.586
of them carries an allele for rifampicin resistance.

00:05:03.227 --> 00:05:06.186
After five days of treatment with rifampicin alone,

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.746
most surviving bacteria carry this allele.

00:05:09.387 --> 00:05:12.506
A student claims, "The rifampicin caused the bacteria

00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:14.426
to mutate so they could survive."

00:05:14.987 --> 00:05:16.186
Evaluate this claim.

00:05:16.747 --> 00:05:17.946
Pause here to answer.

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:29.966
The claim is wrong.

00:05:30.207 --> 00:05:33.726
Before treatment, about a hundred bacteria already carried

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:36.606
the allele: ten billion times one in a

00:05:36.687 --> 00:05:37.766
hundred million.

00:05:38.047 --> 00:05:41.566
So the allele came first, from random mutation.

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:44.686
The rifampicin killed the susceptible bacteria, while the

00:05:44.767 --> 00:05:47.326
resistant ones survived and divided.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:49.486
Rifampicin selected the allele.

00:05:49.567 --> 00:05:51.086
It didn't create it.

00:05:51.127 --> 00:05:53.726
Saying the bacteria mutated because they needed to

00:05:53.967 --> 00:05:55.486
is the classic mistake.

00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:57.260
Second.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.220
A population of lizards lives on an island.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.060
Three events happen.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.620
One: a base change in a cell that

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.020
will make sperm produces a version of a

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.060
scale colour gene never recorded in the species.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.660
Two: an offspring has a combination of existing

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.060
alleles not seen in either parent.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:19.340
Three: the same kind of base change happens

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:20.940
in one lizard's skin cell.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.740
For each event, state whether it can add

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:25.900
a new allele to the next generation's gene

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.740
pool, and name the process.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.660
Pause here to answer.

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:41.826
Event one: yes.

00:06:42.227 --> 00:06:44.066
It's a mutation in a cell that makes

00:06:44.107 --> 00:06:47.106
sperm, so offspring can inherit the new allele.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:49.666
Event two: no.

00:06:49.907 --> 00:06:53.426
Meiosis and fertilisation only reshuffled alleles that were

00:06:53.627 --> 00:06:54.506
already there.

00:06:54.787 --> 00:06:56.746
Event three: no.

00:06:56.947 --> 00:06:59.666
A skin cell mutation can affect that lizard,

00:06:59.747 --> 00:07:01.026
but it isn't passed on.

00:07:01.747 --> 00:07:04.306
If you said every mutation adds an allele

00:07:04.347 --> 00:07:06.466
to the gene pool, check which cell it's

00:07:06.547 --> 00:07:06.946
in.

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:10.566
So when a question asks how a population

00:07:10.647 --> 00:07:13.206
gets a new allele, trace it in order.

00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:16.246
A mutation changes the base sequence in one

00:07:16.567 --> 00:07:17.366
individual.

00:07:17.407 --> 00:07:20.486
It's passed on, by cell division in bacteria,

00:07:20.567 --> 00:07:23.126
or through eggs and sperm in animals.

00:07:23.247 --> 00:07:26.566
Then selection, chance and gene flow change how

00:07:26.647 --> 00:07:28.246
common it becomes.

00:07:28.407 --> 00:07:31.286
Next, you'll see how mutagens make DNA damage

00:07:31.367 --> 00:07:32.246
more likely.

00:07:33.467 --> 00:07:34.906
Here are the key notes.

00:07:35.147 --> 00:07:37.066
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:37.147 --> 00:07:37.946
to copy them.
